      "question": "What is the benefit of replacing NEON kernels with SVE/SVE2 in pixel‑level operations on ARM64?",
      "answer": "Replacing NEON kernels with SVE/SVE2 allows the code to process a larger vector width that scales with the hardware, reducing loop overhead and improving instruction throughput. In the benchmarked SSD, SA8D, VAR, and Hadamard AC kernels on a Yitian 710 CPU, the SVE implementations ran as fast as or faster than NEON, with speedups ranging from 1.2× to 3.8× depending on the block size. For example, ssd_4x16_sve completed in 313 cycles versus 653 for NEON (a 2.1× improvement), and hadamard_ac_16x16_sve finished in 1622 cycles versus 2187 for NEON (a 1.35× improvement). On a Graviton3 instance the gains were similar, with ssd_8x8_sve taking 353 cycles compared to 413 for NEON (1.17×). These results show that SVE/SVE2 can deliver substantial performance benefits for pixel‑wise kernels by exploiting wider vector registers and reduced loop control.\n#### SVE/SVE2 can replace NEON for pixel kernels, giving 1.2×–3.8× speedups depending on block size.",
