      "question": "How can filling a full register before performing lane load instructions reduce dependency chains on ARM64 out‑of‑order CPUs?",
      "answer": "Partial register writes create long dependency chains because later instructions that read the same register must wait for all lanes to be written. By first loading or computing a value that occupies the entire register, subsequent lane‑extracting loads can overwrite only the needed lanes without extending the dependency chain. This technique removes most of the partial‑write hazards in motion‑compensation kernels, leading to measurable speedups. Across a range of Neoverse and Cortex cores the micro‑benchmarks show improvements from roughly 0.88× to 1.03× relative to the previous implementation, with the best case on an A78 core reaching a 1.03× speedup and the worst case on a V2 core at 0.88×.",
      "summary": "Filling registers before lane loads shortens dependency chains and yields 0.88–1.03× speedups on ARM64."
    },
    {
      "question": "When is it advantageous to use scalar FP stores instead of lane‑extracting stores in ARM64 assembly?",
      "answer": "If the store extracts the 0th lane of a vector, it can be replaced by an FP scalar store that writes only the needed value. This eliminates the need to load or manipulate the full vector, reducing instruction count and register pressure. In the benchmark suite for 8‑bit and 16‑bit pixel formats, this optimization produced speedups ranging from about 0.70× to 1.01×, with the most pronounced benefit on an A715 core (up to a 1.01× improvement) and the least on a V2 core (down to 0.70×).",
      "summary": "Using scalar FP stores for 0th‑lane extraction cuts cycles and gives 0.70–1.01× speedups on ARM64."
    }
